cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this material, please cite the archived Zenodo record rather than the repository URL."
title: "Independent replication of both ETH proofs, and a finite range where the SubexponentialSAT bound's exponential factor sits above 2^n with A = 1"
abstract: >-
  Both Lean proofs published against the Exponential Time Hypothesis were rerun locally under the
  official comparator, and both are accepted. For the SubexponentialSAT challenge of openai/math,
  the receipt of a rerun under a real sandbox, with the three limits of that run stated and the raw
  log included; that run used Lean's default kernel, so it is not a check by a second kernel
  implementation. For the different proof published by anthropics/formal-math, the full verification
  their own CI runs, whose configuration enables the nanoda kernel: there two independently written
  kernels accept the same proof, which closes the first of those three limits. Their reference
  statement is 17 lines and the file defining its meaning is 182, with definitions only, so it was
  also read by hand, and the input-encoding question left open for openai/math closes there. Also, a
  recomputation of the openai/math time bound: taking A = 1, the ratio runtimeScale(n)/n is not
  monotone, so the exponential factor sits above 2^n over a finite range of n. Both crossings of
  that range are closed-form in the Lambert W function, at n = 49.175096 on the principal branch
  and near 10^2390.21 on branch -1, with the maximum 16/e^2 at L = e^4. The finite range has no
  asymptotic consequence, and this material refutes nothing and endorses nothing: accepting a proof
  is not endorsing a claim, and for anthropics/formal-math the bridge from Mathlib's machine type to
  the usual notion of algorithm is, by their own statement, outside what Lean checked.
authors:
  - family-names: Busnello
    given-names: Luiz Antonio
    affiliation: "Independent Researcher, São Paulo, Brazil"
type: software
license: MIT
date-released: 2026-10-31
# The concept DOI is stable across every release, so it is the one that can honestly live inside a
# snapshot. It was minted with the v1.0.0 release on 2026-10-30 and added here afterwards, which is
# why the v1.0.0 archive itself does not carry it: no first release can contain a DOI that does not
# exist yet. Each release also receives its own version DOI: v1.0.0 is 10.5281/zenodo.23820112,
# v1.0.1, which added the PDF, is 10.5281/zenodo.23821317, v1.0.2 is 10.5281/zenodo.23821421, and
# v1.1.0, the first to carry both replications, is 10.5281/zenodo.23836157. Find those on the
# Zenodo record.
doi: 10.5281/zenodo.23820111
repository-code: "https://github.com/totobusnello/eth-subexp-note"
keywords:
